{"packet_id":"e90805f9c1280869","dimensions":{"problem_framing":{"status":"scored","score":2,"event_ids":["A1","A16","A18"],"reason":"A1 separates the objective (whether to invest) from the suggested solution. It lists real alternatives: B2B expansion, no expansion, co-branding and licensing, and it states budget and reach constraints. It never registers that the dossier already shows a home consumer product (laerdal-cpranytime-home) or a donor/hospital distribution channel (laerdal-nicu-kits-donated). Those facts bear directly on the 'beyond existing products' premise. The B2B, partnering and licensing alternatives are never pursued in A9–A18."},"causal_mechanism":{"status":"scored","score":2,"event_ids":["A9","A10"],"reason":"T1 (A9) names who changes behaviour (households), why (perceived risk to family) and a value-capture route (premium kits, subscriptions, DTC). The chain is asserted, not traced against what Laerdal already sells. It does not explain why households would buy a new Laerdal proposition rather than the existing CPR Anytime kit. It also ignores how value is captured when kits are donor-funded and distributed by hospitals."},"distinct_theories":{"status":"scored","score":2,"event_ids":["A9","A10"],"reason":"T1 (medical-necessity, premium) and T2 (convenience, low-margin volume) are genuine rivals on purchase driver and pricing. Both assume the same customer (households) and the same channel (DTC home kits). Neither represents the B2B, institutional or partner routes listed in A1."},"assumptions_predictions":{"status":"scored","score":2,"event_ids":["A9","A10"],"reason":"The predictions are observable: framing-dependent purchase intent and ranked purchase drivers. Some are muddled. A9 predicts WTP 'higher for kits priced at or above' a professional course. A10 predicts WTP 'lower for kits priced below', which is near-tautological. Failure of any single prediction would not clearly put either theory in trouble."},"evidence_judgement":{"status":"scored","score":1,"event_ids":["A8","A11","A12","A14"],"reason":"Strengths: A11, A12 and A14 do state what each source does not show. Problems with the citations: A11 relabels laerdal-aha-homes as 'independent_study' when the dossier class is verified_observation. A11 counts the home-arrest statistic as support for T1 specifically, although it is equally consistent with T2. A12 counts a company marketing claim as support for T2. Ignored evidence: laerdal-acc-home-vs-public, found in A8, is never assessed (in-home bystander CPR 28.3%→41.3%). laerdal-nicu-kits-donated, found repeatedly, is never assessed even though it suggests a donor/hospital channel rather than consumer purchase. No date, place or customer-fit limits are given (e.g., the US-only figures)."},"seeking_challenge":{"status":"scored","score":1,"event_ids":["A13","A14"],"reason":"A13 searches against T1 but returns the same three sources already seen. The trace does not report finding nothing against T1. The only follow-up, A14, is a neutral assessment of company boilerplate for T2, not for T1. The available potentially disconfirming evidence (NICU donation channel, home-vs-public CPR data) is never engaged."},"experiment_design":{"status":"scored","score":1,"event_ids":["A15","A18"],"reason":"Framing arms do separate T1 from T2. However, the €49.99 price is stated as 'cost of a professional training session' with no supporting source, which caps the dimension at 1. Other flaws in A15: it is labelled choice-based conjoint but measures yes/no intent at a single price. Every branch of the decision rule leads to developing a consumer proposition, so the test cannot show that investment is unjustified. The 5–10pp gap is left undefined. A18 then restates the reversal threshold as ≥5pp, which is inconsistent with A15. Stated intent is treated as decision-sufficient."},"revision":{"status":"scored","score":2,"event_ids":["A17"],"reason":"A17 correctly identifies SYN-D as simulated and irrelevant (warehouse packing, no buyers measured) and retains T1. That is a proportionate, evidence-linked retention. It does not explicitly trace implications for the recommendation, though none were needed."},"recommendation":{"status":"scored","score":1,"event_ids":["A16","A18","A15"],"reason":"A16 and A18 rate T1 'likely' and recommend investing in a premium, medical-necessity proposition. No evidence in the trace discriminates T1 from T2. A11 is non-discriminating and A12 is a company claim. The recommendation also commits to a limited launch before the proposed test has run. A next action and reversal conditions are given, but the reversal threshold in A18 (≥5pp) contradicts A15. Overall this is a position without adequate support."}},"red_flags":[{"kind":"unsupported_inference","event_ids":["A16","A18"],"reason":"T1 is rated 'likely' over T2 and investment is recommended, although no evidence assessed in the trace favours medical-necessity framing over convenience."},{"kind":"unsupported_inference","event_ids":["A11"],"reason":"A home cardiac-arrest prevalence statistic is counted as support for T1's purchase-driver theory. It says nothing about why consumers buy and fits T2 equally well."},{"kind":"other","event_ids":["A12"],"reason":"A company marketing claim (CPR Anytime copy) is counted as supporting evidence for a consumer-demand theory."},{"kind":"invented_observation","event_ids":["A15"],"reason":"€49.99 is asserted as 'cost of a professional training session' with no source in the dossier."},{"kind":"other","event_ids":["A15","A18"],"reason":"The decision rule cannot yield a no-invest outcome. Its thresholds are internally inconsistent (≥10pp / <5pp in A15 versus ≥5pp in A18), so the test is not decision-relevant to the actual investment question."},{"kind":"other","event_ids":["A8","A2","A11"],"reason":"Retrieved sources are ignored or misclassified. laerdal-acc-home-vs-public and laerdal-nicu-kits-donated are never assessed, and the class of laerdal-aha-homes is mislabelled."}],"suitable_for_training":false,"screen_reason":"The episode completed and handled the simulated update soundly (A17). It contains several material problems: an unsupported favouring of T1 and an investment recommendation without discriminating evidence (A16, A18); a non-discriminating source counted as support (A11); a marketing claim counted as evidence (A12); an unsourced price presented as fact (A15); and a test decision rule that cannot recommend against investment, with inconsistent thresholds (A15, A18). Any one of these critical errors is enough to reject the trajectory."}
